--- fig-U4-5.svg (27 text elements) ---
  1 Choose ONE
  indicator
  not a standard, one indicator
  2 Collect
  evidence
  of practice as it is now
  3 Judge the
  gap honestly
  against the indicator, not a peer
  4 Plan ONE
  change
  small enough to do next week
  5 Re-collect the
  SAME evidence
  or the comparison means nothing
  same indicator again, or the next one
  Where it breaks, first
  Choosing too many indicators at once. Five indicators
  produce five half-measurements and no change you
  could attribute to anything.
  Where it breaks, second
  Changing the evidence rather than the practice. If
  station 5 measures something easier than station 2,
  the loop will always show improvement.
  The loop is cheap. What makes it work is that station 5 repeats station 2 exactly, and that station 1 was
  honest about which indicator to pick.
  textbook.com.pk
